WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.322
This slide focuses on one rule that explains many SQL surprises: WHERE keeps only TRUE.

00:00:06.322 --> 00:00:10.082
The example contains three Boolean states: true, false, and NULL.

00:00:10.082 --> 00:00:15.737
When we use the Boolean field directly in a WHERE clause, only the true row remains.

00:00:15.737 --> 00:00:19.942
The false row is excluded, which feels natural.

00:00:19.942 --> 00:00:24.107
The NULL row is also excluded because NULL behaves as UNKNOWN.

00:00:24.107 --> 00:00:29.442
SQL cannot prove that the condition is true.

00:00:29.442 --> 00:00:32.402
Now think about a nullable consent field.

00:00:32.402 --> 00:00:37.892
If we write WHERE consent, only patients with explicit true consent remain.

00:00:37.892 --> 00:00:43.652
Patients with false consent are removed, and patients with unknown consent are also removed.

00:00:43.652 --> 00:00:47.237
That may be exactly what we want for a messaging campaign,

00:00:47.237 --> 00:00:51.432
because we should not assume permission when consent has not been recorded.

00:00:51.432 --> 00:00:59.722
But imagine a data-quality report whose purpose is to find anyone who has not explicitly consented.

00:00:59.722 --> 00:01:03.042
In that case, false and NULL may both be important.

00:01:03.042 --> 00:01:07.167
A simple WHERE consent would not answer that question.

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:12.697
The lesson is that WHERE has a very strict gate: only TRUE passes.

00:01:12.697 --> 00:01:18.337
Before filtering on a nullable condition, decide what you want to do with unknown values.

00:01:18.337 --> 00:01:22.332
Exclude them, include them, or label them separately.

00:01:22.332 --> 00:01:27.009
Make that policy explicit instead of letting SQL choose it accidentally.
